Short explanations for the 'skyax' package: (status 3. June 2026)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

The following instructions assume that you are on a Linux system.

Create a working directory 'SkyAx' and change to it. Place the
the package 'SkyAx.tgz' there and unpack it. This will generate
the documentation file "Skyax.pdf" and the subdirectories:
source_f90         contains all source files and a 'Makefile', the
                   latter provides options for GNU Fortran and
                   Intel Fortran;
doc-doxygen        contains sources for 'doxygen' and its actual output
                   on 'SkyAx_doxygen.pdf' which delivers more detailed
                   information from the various subroutines.
                   (The doxygen output has yet to be updated.)
test_cases_Skyrme  Skyrme test cases input and output.
test_cases_Fayans  Fayans test cases input and output.

To compile and link change to subdirectory 'source_f90' and edit the
'Makefile'. Two compilers are provided, Intel Fortran 'ifort' and
GNU Fortran 'gfortran'. Check the 'COMPILERFLAGS' such that they
comply with your installation.
Once you have updated the 'Makefile' just issue a 'make'. That
will tell you the usage. For example, if you want to use 'gfortran'
just execute 'make ifort'. This will place an executable
'skyax.ifort.seq' in the working directory (one level above 'source').

A simple way to run the code is to choose one of the functionals
provided in the file 'source_f90/forces.data'. Examples for input are
given in the subdirectories of the directories 'test_cases_*' with name
starting by 'for005'. All these test cases contains input files and
corresponding output files to check the actual installation.

To specify an example:  take 'Cd118' where the input file is named
'for005'. Go to a working directory of your choice, copy 'for005' from
'Ca48' to that and run '~/SkyAx/skyax.ifort.seq < for005'. This
will produce several output files with the qualifier 'tst' (defined in
'for005') from which the 'ensurf.tst' contains compressed information
on deformation, energy, radii etc. The file 'out_details.tst' shows a
detailed protocol of iteration and results. For more explanations and
the meaning of other output files see the documentation in 'SkyAx.pdf'.


There is still one obnoxious feature. The results depend slightly on
the compiler used. The worst case is Intel Fortran with OpenMP
(option 'make ifort_omp') which can fail badly in some cases. This
indicates that there is probably some non-standard use of Fortran
constructs which deserves further checking.


Large-scale calculations: mass table and stable-line (dripline) scan
=====================================================================

Three run modes are selected via 'run_mode' in the &main namelist.

  run_mode=0  Single nucleus (default, original SkyAx behaviour)
  run_mode=1  Mass table -- loop over a list of (Z, N, beta0) entries
  run_mode=2  Stable-line scan -- follow isotopic/isotonic chains to the dripline


Supporting files
----------------

  m_masstable.f90        Module that reads input, stores results, and writes
                         the summary output file for modes 1 and 2.
  skyax_MASSTABLE.dat    Input for run_mode=1: list of (Z, N, beta0) entries.
  skyax_STABLELINE.dat   Input for run_mode=2: list of chain starting points.
  test/masstable/        Sample input and output for run_mode=1.
  test/stableline/       Sample input and output for run_mode=2.


Output files
------------

Each nucleus computed in modes 1 and 2 is assigned a 4-digit sequential
index (0001, 0002, ...) and gets its own set of output files:

  out_details/out_details.0001
  ensurf/ensurf.0001
  conver/conver.0001
  radii/radii.0001
  ...

These are the same files that single-nucleus mode writes, but named by
index rather than by the 'outnam' qualifier.  All subdirectories are
created automatically before the loop starts.

At the end of the run a single summary file is written:

  MassTableOut.dat     (run_mode=1)
  StableLineOut.dat    (run_mode=2)

Each row of the summary contains the index, Z, N, beta_in, and the
converged observables (deformation, energy, pairing, Fermi energies,
gaps, radii, bound nucleon numbers, energy variance, dE/diter).


Controlling odd-A and odd-odd nuclei
-------------------------------------

Two logical flags in &main govern whether nuclei with odd Z or N are
computed.  Both default to .TRUE. (all nuclei included).

  mt_do_oddA   = .TRUE.   Include odd-A nuclei   (Z+N odd, exactly one is odd)
  mt_do_oddodd = .TRUE.   Include odd-odd nuclei (both Z and N are odd)

Set either flag to .FALSE. to skip those nuclei.  The blocking machinery
(m_blocking.f90) handles odd-nucleon configurations automatically whenever
a nucleus with odd Z or N is encountered.

For run_mode=1 the flags act as a row filter: rows whose (Z, N) parity
does not match are skipped with a log message and do not appear in
MassTableOut.dat.

For run_mode=2 the flags control whether the intermediate odd neighbor is
computed between each pair of same-parity reference nuclei (see below).


run_mode=0 -- Single nucleus
------------------------------

Standard usage; no additional input files are needed.

  &main outnam='tst', itmax=10000, run_mode=0, ...
  /

Output files carry the 'outnam' qualifier (e.g. ensurf.tst, out_details.tst).


run_mode=1 -- Mass table
-------------------------

Reads 'skyax_MASSTABLE.dat' from the working directory (see e.g. in test folder).

File format (header line, total-row count, column-label line, then one row per nucleus):

  SkyAx mass-table input file
  24
       Z     N    beta0
       8     8     0.00
       9    10     0.00
      50    82     0.00
      ...

Each row specifies Z, N and the initial deformation beta0.  Odd-A and
odd-odd nuclei are included by default (controlled by mt_do_oddA and
mt_do_oddodd).

Example namelist (&main block only; the rest of for005 is unchanged):

  &main outnam='mt', iprint=500, itmax=10000,
        run_mode=1,
        mt_start=1, mt_end=-1,
        mt_do_oddA=.TRUE.,
        mt_do_oddodd=.TRUE.,
  /

mt_start / mt_end select a row range within the file (mt_end=-1 means all
rows).  For SLURM array jobs set these so that each array element processes
a disjoint range:

  sbatch --array=1-10 run_masstable.sbatch

The summary is written to 'MassTableOut.dat' when the run finishes.


run_mode=2 -- Stable-line (dripline) scan
------------------------------------------

Reads 'skyax_STABLELINE.dat' from the working directory.

File format (header line, chain count, column-label line, then one row per chain):

  SkyAx stable-line (dripline scan) input file
  2
             Z           N   direction
             8           8           1
            50          50          -1

direction=1  isotopic chain: fix Z, scan N upward
direction=-1 isotonic chain: fix N, scan Z upward

The code starts each chain at the given (Z, N) and steps by 2 (in N for
isotopic, in Z for isotonic) until the 2-nucleon separation energy becomes
negative:

  S_2N = E(Z, N_prev) - E(Z, N_cur) < 0  =>  neutron dripline reached
  S_2Z = E(Z_prev, N) - E(Z_cur, N) < 0  =>  proton  dripline reached

Odd-nucleus interleaving (mt_do_oddA / mt_do_oddodd):

When odd nuclei are enabled (the default), after each reference nucleus
passes the dripline check the immediate odd neighbor is computed before
advancing to the next reference nucleus:

  Isotopic chain (fixed Z):
    (Z, N) -> [S_2N check] -> (Z, N+1) -> advance to (Z, N+2) -> ...

  Isotonic chain (fixed N):
    (Z, N) -> [S_2N check] -> (Z+1, N) -> advance to (Z+2, N) -> ...

The dripline check always compares consecutive same-parity reference nuclei,
so odd-even staggering cannot cause a spurious early termination.

Whether the intermediate neighbor is odd-A or odd-odd depends on the parity
of the fixed leg (Z for isotopic, N for isotonic):

  even fixed leg  =>  odd-A neighbor   => governed by mt_do_oddA
  odd  fixed leg  =>  odd-odd neighbor => governed by mt_do_oddodd

Example namelist:

  &main outnam='FR0', title='dripline scan', itmax=10000,
        iprint=1000, tpri_conver=.TRUE.,
        run_mode=2,
        mt_do_oddA=.TRUE.,
        mt_do_oddodd=.TRUE.,
  /

The summary is written to 'StableLineOut.dat' when the run finishes. Examples are put in test folder. 
